## Why This Is Asked

Tests end-to-end exactly-once thinking across DB, Kafka, and Redis in high-throughput CNPA.

## Key Concepts

- Idempotency keys
- Outbox pattern
- Kafka transactions
- Redis cache invalidation
- Dedup store and replay protection
- Observability and load testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo transactional flow for exactly-once
// 1) write to outbox atomically with main write
db.insertOutbox(eventId, payload, false);
db.commitTransaction();

// 2) publish via Kafka using transactions
producer.initTransactions();
producer.beginTransaction();
producer.send({ topic: 'events', key: eventId, value: payload });
producer.commitTransaction();

// 3) mark outbox as published and invalidate cache
db.markOutboxPublished(eventId);
db.commitTransaction();
redis.invalidate(eventId);
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate end-to-end exactly-once under load and define guardrails for replay?
- What metrics and rollback criteria would you use to detect and recover from dedup store growth or Kafka transaction failures?

## Why This Is Asked
Aimed to assess mastery of end-to-end guarantees across HTTP API, DB, Kafka, and Redis, including failure handling and read-model integrity.

## Key Concepts
- Outbox pattern
- Kafka transactional producers
- Idempotent consumers
- Cross-service failure handling
- Read model consistency and testing

## Code Example
```javascript
// PostgreSQL transaction: write user update and outbox row
BEGIN;
UPDATE users SET name = $1 WHERE id = $2;
INSERT INTO outbox (aggregate_type, aggregate_id, event_type, payload, sent)
VALUES ('user', $2, 'UserUpdated', '{id:' + $2 + ',name:' + $1 + '}', false);
COMMIT;
```

```javascript
// Pseudo publisher
async function publishOutbox() {
  const rows = await fetchUnsentOutbox();
  const txn = await kafka.beginTransaction();
  for (const r of rows) {
    await kafka.send({ topic: 'user.events', key: r.aggregate_id, value: r.payload }, txn);
  }
  await markOutboxSent(rows.map(r => r.id));
  await txn.commit();
}
```

